1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,290
Right now, a machine built in 1977 is still

2
00:00:06,290 --> 00:00:09,420
sending us messages from more than twenty-four

3
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:11,595
billion kilometres away.

4
00:00:11,595 --> 00:00:14,585
And on Mars, another machine has said nothing

5
00:00:14,585 --> 00:00:16,704
at all for eight years.

6
00:00:16,704 --> 00:00:19,034
Same agency. Same promise.

7
00:00:19,034 --> 00:00:21,146
Two very different endings.

8
00:00:21,146 --> 00:00:23,100
I'm Md Mahiyan Noor Mahi,

9
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:26,441
and this is Team Cosmic Eclipse from Sylhet,

10
00:00:26,441 --> 00:00:27,861
Bangladesh.

11
00:00:27,861 --> 00:00:29,758
My teammates are: Maliha,

12
00:00:29,758 --> 00:00:31,908
team leader; Nuhza, designer;

13
00:00:31,908 --> 00:00:33,615
Fatema, data scientist;

14
00:00:33,615 --> 00:00:35,386
Developer, Omi; Montaha,

15
00:00:35,386 --> 00:00:37,157
researcher; and finally,

16
00:00:37,157 --> 00:00:39,887
me, Mahi, the storyteller.

17
00:00:39,887 --> 00:00:41,997
We kept asking one question:

18
00:00:41,997 --> 00:00:43,517
when a mission goes quiet,

19
00:00:43,517 --> 00:00:45,593
where does its story go?

20
00:00:45,593 --> 00:00:48,323
Our answer is a website called Abandoned,

21
00:00:48,323 --> 00:00:50,204
But Not Forgotten.

22
00:00:50,204 --> 00:00:54,084
Since the 1960s, NASA has left hardware on

23
00:00:54,084 --> 00:00:57,174
the Moon, on Mars, and in deep space.

24
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:00,440
The facts exist. But they're scattered across

25
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:02,550
mission pages, press releases,

26
00:01:02,550 --> 00:01:03,720
and PDFs.

27
00:01:04,351 --> 00:01:06,311
And one word hides the truth:

28
00:01:06,311 --> 00:01:07,321
abandoned.

29
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:10,580
Nobody drove these machines home.

30
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:13,172
They worked until something stopped them.

31
00:01:13,172 --> 00:01:14,545
Dust.

32
00:01:14,545 --> 00:01:15,961
Cold.

33
00:01:15,961 --> 00:01:17,825
Fading power.

34
00:01:17,825 --> 00:01:18,855
Distance.

35
00:01:19,619 --> 00:01:22,329
Opportunity was built to last ninety Martian

36
00:01:22,329 --> 00:01:26,256
days. It worked for more than fourteen years.

37
00:01:26,256 --> 00:01:30,146
Then, in 2018, a global dust storm blocked

38
00:01:30,146 --> 00:01:31,427
its sunlight.

39
00:01:31,427 --> 00:01:34,037
Engineers sent more than a thousand recovery

40
00:01:34,037 --> 00:01:37,018
commands. Nothing came back.

41
00:01:37,018 --> 00:01:39,038
That's the part that matters most:

42
00:01:39,038 --> 00:01:40,588
why a mission ended.

43
00:01:40,588 --> 00:01:42,938
And it's the part hardest to find.

44
00:01:43,721 --> 00:01:46,711
A student shouldn't need ten tabs to understand it.

45
00:01:47,349 --> 00:01:49,379
So we built one place for it.

46
00:01:49,379 --> 00:01:52,999
Fifteen missions, from 1966 to today,

47
00:01:52,999 --> 00:01:56,639
across the Moon, Mars and deep space.

48
00:01:56,639 --> 00:01:58,349
Scroll the homepage,

49
00:01:58,349 --> 00:02:01,099
and the story unfolds scene by scene,

50
00:02:01,099 --> 00:02:04,073
from leaving Earth to the final silence.

51
00:02:04,073 --> 00:02:06,743
Follow six decades on the timeline,

52
00:02:06,743 --> 00:02:09,864
or find every machine on the hardware map.

53
00:02:09,864 --> 00:02:12,664
Our Final Signal page separates four moments

54
00:02:12,664 --> 00:02:15,154
most people mix up: launch,

55
00:02:15,154 --> 00:02:17,214
arrival, last contact,

56
00:02:17,214 --> 00:02:18,733
and official closure.

57
00:02:18,733 --> 00:02:22,476
Because lost and finished are not the same thing.

58
00:02:22,476 --> 00:02:26,091
Pick a spacecraft and turn it around in 3D.

59
00:02:26,091 --> 00:02:28,101
Or play Mission Detective.

60
00:02:28,101 --> 00:02:29,801
Read the clues about Spirit,

61
00:02:29,801 --> 00:02:32,091
Opportunity, or Surveyor 3,

62
00:02:32,091 --> 00:02:34,101
and work out what happened.

63
00:02:34,101 --> 00:02:36,261
There's a quiz, a mission comparison,

64
00:02:36,261 --> 00:02:38,420
and a 3D game too.

65
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:42,383
Every fact comes from NASA and JPL sources.

66
00:02:42,383 --> 00:02:45,283
And when a machine speaks in first person,

67
00:02:45,283 --> 00:02:48,593
we label it clearly as creative interpretation,

68
00:02:48,593 --> 00:02:51,132
never as a real transmission.

69
00:02:51,132 --> 00:02:52,592
Why does this matter?

70
00:02:53,781 --> 00:02:56,471
For a student, it turns space history from

71
00:02:56,471 --> 00:02:58,711
a list of dates into a set of stories with

72
00:02:58,711 --> 00:03:00,861
an ending, and a reason.

73
00:03:01,955 --> 00:03:04,335
It's built with plain HTML,

74
00:03:04,335 --> 00:03:06,995
CSS, and JavaScript.

75
00:03:06,995 --> 00:03:08,815
No framework, no backend,

76
00:03:08,815 --> 00:03:10,725
and most of it works offline.

77
00:03:11,522 --> 00:03:13,692
So a classroom with a weak connection can

78
00:03:13,692 --> 00:03:16,842
still open it. That matters where we live.

79
00:03:18,007 --> 00:03:21,087
And it's honest. Sources are on the site,

80
00:03:21,087 --> 00:03:23,577
and the line between fact and imagination

81
00:03:23,577 --> 00:03:24,987
is never blurred.

82
00:03:26,036 --> 00:03:27,266
This is a first version,

83
00:03:27,266 --> 00:03:28,996
with fifteen missions.

84
00:03:28,996 --> 00:03:31,526
To take it further, we need three things:

85
00:03:32,388 --> 00:03:35,098
experts to review and expand the archive,

86
00:03:36,077 --> 00:03:39,957
access to more NASA imagery and 3D surface data,

87
00:03:40,871 --> 00:03:42,061
and a Bangla version,

88
00:03:42,061 --> 00:03:43,731
with teacher resources,

89
00:03:43,731 --> 00:03:45,611
so schools here can use it.

90
00:03:48,056 --> 00:03:50,046
Voyager is still talking.

91
00:03:50,046 --> 00:03:51,816
Opportunity went quiet.

92
00:03:52,610 --> 00:03:55,310
Our job is to make sure both are heard.

93
00:03:56,366 --> 00:03:58,296
Abandoned, But Not Forgotten.
